tale ones over a day old) and, when an upload or import fails to publish, the empty file it had reserved for it. It never follows links out of those folders, and its file API never exposes the agent's Hermes home apart from a working folder set inside it (roots, terminal.cwd, or a workspace/ folder it creates when the working folder would be the home itself). Download, drag and Add to chat use Hermes Desktop's own authenticated file download and @file/@folder references; the plugin adds no route for them. Desktop listens for the agent's tool-completion events to refresh the open folder, and its Desktop windows pass each such event's type and agent to one another in the app (a BroadcastChannel; nothing leaves the machine). In Desktop chats only, it adds a short section to the agent's instructions that names the shown folders (at most ten, as configured) and explains the ::cloud-file{path=...} card; it carries no file names or contents. A card's path goes only to a read-only lookup route under the same folder and Hermes-home guards as a listing; a path outside the shown folders is refused. Each write names the folder it started in, and the gateway refuses it if Settings have since changed which folder that is. Apart from Desktop's calls to the gateway and the report skill's GitHub requests, its only network use is Google Drive, read-only, through the agent's own google-workspace skill, whose scripts (from skills/productivity/google-workspace in the agent's Hermes home, else the copy bundled with Hermes) it runs with the gateway's Python and a minimal environment without API keys, buffering their output in the gateway's temp directory; the plugin never reads the Google token. While Desktop is open it re-checks the plugin and Drive sign-in about once a minute. The report skill runs hermes --version, hermes plugins list and hermes logs (filtered to this plugin), may save its search words, title and draft as local files, and searches electricsheephq/hermes-cloud-file-mana